    "case_id": "3",
    "case_summary": "A 43-year-old woman with a long-standing history of schizophrenia since 1982, and previous hospitalization aimed at improving social communication skills at the age of 23, reported experiencing mild pain in her left flank and back. In 1995, she underwent ureterolysis and ureteroureterostomy due to left-sided ureteral obstruction. Histological examination of the biopsy showed benign fibrous tissue proliferations. No additional evaluation or surgical removal was planned at that time due to her deteriorating mental condition. She was placed under regular follow-up with imaging studies. In May 2002, she was referred from the psychiatric hospital to the Department of Urology as the mass exhibited growth.\n\nCT scan with contrast enhancement revealed a tumor located between iliocostalis and psoas muscles in retroperitoneal space. The peripheral part of the tumor was enhanced, while the central part did not. The left paravertebral muscles around the tumor showed atrophy. The medial margin of the tumor was deformed by a left transverse process of the second lumber spine. Coronal magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) demonstrated the tumor to be located beneath the left kidney. The central part of the tumor was found to be of iso/low intensity in the T1-weighted phase and of heterogeneously high intensity in the T2-weighted phase. The marginal part showed very low intensity in both phases. Needle biopsy revealed that the tumor consisted of well-proliferated spindle cells rich in collagen fibers.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
